 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 58,
   "y": 25,
   "plaque": "penzance_poldhu",
   "title": "Poldhu",
   "text": "Across Mount's Bay, at Poldhu on the Lizard, Guglielmo Marconi built a giant radio transmitter. On 12 December 1901, on Signal Hill in Newfoundland, he said he heard three faint clicks, the Morse letter S, sent from Poldhu across the Atlantic. Some still doubt he really did. Within a few years, nobody doubted radio."
  },
  {
   "text": "SIR HUMPHRY DAVY. Born in Penzance, 1778. He invented the miner's safety lamp, which saved countless lives. His statue stands on the street where he lost a few marbles as a boy.",
   "x": 34.0,
   "y": 23.0
  },
  {
   "text": "THE ADMIRAL BENBOW. A pub on Chapel Street, with a ship's figurehead, a smuggler's past, and a landlord who has heard every Treasure Island joke.",
   "x": 40.0,
   "y": 19.0
  },
  {
   "text": "GEEVOR TIN MINE. Working until 1990, now a museum. Miners dug tin and copper from tunnels running out under the sea. You can hear the waves overhead. Hard hats provided. Jokes not.",
   "x": 9.0,
   "y": 25.0
  },
  {
   "text": "CORNWALL'S MINING LANDSCAPE. A World Heritage Site since 2006. Cornish engine houses dot the coast, each one a chimney with a story.",
   "x": 20.0,
   "y": 25.0
  },
  {
   "text": "THE JUBILEE POOL. Built in 1935, a triangular art deco seawater lido, and one of the biggest in Britain. It's cold. It's gorgeous. It's cold.",
   "x": 10.0,
   "y": 31.0
  },
  {
   "text": "PENZANCE HARBOUR. Pen Sans in Cornish: 'holy headland'. Penzance has been a harbour since the Middle Ages. A Spanish raiding party burned it in 1595. Now only the seagulls raid it.",
   "x": 25.0,
   "y": 36.0
  },
  {
   "text": "THE CAUSEWAY. At low tide you can walk to St Michael's Mount. At high tide you can join the fishes. Check the board. The board is truthful.",
   "x": 48.0,
   "y": 34.0
  },
  {
   "text": "ST MICHAEL'S MOUNT. A tidal island with a castle on top and a village at its feet. In Cornish it's Karrek Loos yn Koos, the 'grey rock in the wood'. The wood went under the sea ages ago. The rock stayed.",
   "x": 53.0,
   "y": 33.0
  },
  {
   "x": 1,
   "y": 19,
   "text": "Road to Land's End closed for resurfacing. Again. Sorry for any inconvenience."
  },
  {
   "x": 62,
   "y": 19,
   "text": "Road to Truro closed for resurfacing. Again. Sorry for any inconvenience."
  },
  {
   "x": 48,
   "y": 17,
   "text": "BLUE PLAQUE: Sir Humphry Davy, chemist and inventor of the miners' safety lamp, was born in Penzance in 1778.",
   "kind": "plaque",
   "dx": -0.475,
   "plaque": "penzance_davy",
   "title": "Sir Humphry Davy"
  },
  {
   "x": 50,
   "y": 18,
   "plaque": "penzance_maria_branwell",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "Maria Branwell",
   "text": "Maria Branwell was born on Chapel Street in 1783. She married a curate called Patrick Bronte and became the mother of Charlotte, Emily and Anne. The Bronte sisters wrote about Yorkshire moors, but their mum grew up beside the sea."
  },
  {
   "x": 38,
   "y": 24,
   "plaque": "penzance_dolly_pentreath",
   "title": "Dolly Pentreath",
   "text": "Dolly Pentreath of Mousehole, who died in 1777, is often called the last native speaker of Cornish, though a few others spoke it after her. The language never quite went away. Today Kernewek, Cornish in Cornish, is taught again in schools and evening classes."
  },
  {
   "x": 43,
   "y": 30,
   "plaque": "penzance_newlyn_school",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "The Newlyn School",
   "text": "In the 1880s a band of painters led by Stanhope Forbes settled in Newlyn, just round the bay, to paint fishing families out of doors in the clear Cornish light. They became known as the Newlyn School. The light is still free. The paint costs extra."
  },
  {
   "x": 23,
   "y": 35,
   "plaque": "penzance_zennor_mermaid",
   "title": "Mermaid of Zennor",
   "text": "Up the coast at Zennor, legend tells of a mermaid who fell for Mathew Trewella, the best singer in the church choir, and lured him into the sea. A carved mermaid still decorates an old bench end in the church. On calm evenings, they say, you can hear him singing."
  },
  {
   "x": 40,
   "y": 24,
   "plaque": "penzance_kernewek_revival",
   "title": "Kernewek Reborn",
   "local": {
    "kw": "Kernewek"
   },
   "text": "In 1904 Henry Jenner published his Handbook of the Cornish Language, and a revival began. Gorsedh Kernow, the Cornish bardic society, was founded in 1928. Cornish was recognised under the European Charter for Regional or Minority Languages in 2002, and UNESCO moved it from extinct to critically endangered in 2010. Dydh da means good day."
  },
  {
   "x": 44,
   "y": 24,
   "plaque": "arts_hepworth",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "Barbara Hepworth",
   "text": "Barbara Hepworth, born in Wakefield in 1903, came to St Ives, just over the hill, in 1939, and stayed. From 1949 she carved and cast at Trewyn Studio, pierced stone and bronze forms with the sea in them; her Single Form stands outside the United Nations in New York. She died in a fire at the studio in 1975, and it opened as a museum the next year, with her garden kept as she left it. Tate St Ives followed in 1993. Do not touch the sculptures. They are allowed to touch you."
  },
  {
   "x": 14,
   "y": 10,
   "plaque": "landsky_gulf_stream",
   "title": "The warm water",
   "text": "Penzance is as far north as the frozen coast of Labrador, yet frost is rare and palms grow on the promenade. The reason lies out at sea: the Gulf Stream carries warm water up from the Gulf of Mexico, and its continuation, the North Atlantic Drift, crosses the ocean to wash the west of these isles. It is why Edinburgh is milder in winter than Moscow, which lies at much the same latitude, and why gardens from Tresco in the Isles of Scilly to Inverewe in Wester Ross grow plants from the other side of the world."
  }
 ],
